I agree with latro'a. {ce'u} is for marking arguments in functions
constructed primarily with the {ka} abstractor. If we use the
property-based lujvo for desire, {kaidji}, then we can use {ce'u} without a
problem:

.i ma kaidji lo ka ce'u ponse lo mi gerku
[what?] desires the property [X owns the (restrictively-associated-with)-me
dog]
Who wants to have my dog?

{ka}-abstractions with only one {ce'u} are sometimes called infinitives for
that reason, namely that they translate to infinitives in English and
similar natural languages.

.i mi'e la tsani mu'o


